(*
expect:
before nil
set 0
after 66600
finished
*)
(* tuvm#100 — setting the RTC, and reading back what was set.

   TWO ARGUMENTS, NOT SIX. Zephyr's rtc_set_time takes a full struct rtc_time, and
   the design (§6) has the phone app write a full date and time, so the seam cannot
   be time-of-day only. But a six-keyword prim would push past the argument counts
   anything else in this VM uses, so the date is packed as YYYYMMDD — 20260731 —
   which is an ordinary decimal a human can read in a log and is comfortably inside
   the 2^30 SMI ceiling (20,260,731 against 1,073,741,824). The time stays as
   seconds-since-midnight, the same unit `wallClock` answers in, so a round trip is
   an identity rather than a conversion.

   ONE call, not a date call followed by a time call: setting a clock in two steps
   means a window where the part holds a date from one instant and a time from
   another, and the second write can land after midnight has moved the day on.

   `before` is nil because a host has no RTC until something sets it — the same
   default 02 asserts. That it becomes readable purely as a RESULT OF THE SET is
   the claim: it distinguishes a seam that stores what it was given from one that
   merely accepts and discards it, which a set-only test could not tell apart. *)
class WallClockSet packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | before rc after |
    before:: platform wallClock.
    ('before ' , before printString) out.

    rc:: platform wallClockSetDate: 20260731 time: 66600.
    ('set ' , rc printString) out.

    after:: platform wallClock.
    ('after ' , after printString) out.
    'finished' out.
  )
) : (
)
